      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Let $a,$ $b,$ $c$ be the roots of $3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0.$  Then by Vieta's formulas, $a + b + c = \\frac{3}{3} = 1,$\n$ab + ac + bc = \\frac{11}{3},$ and $abc = -\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nNow, we want to find $ab + ac + bc.$  We can rewrite the equation $3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0$ as $(x-a)(x-b)(x-c) = 0,$ which means\n\\[3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 18.\\]Thus,\n\\[3abx^2 - 3abcx + abc = 0,\\]so\n\\[3ab(a+b+c) - 3abc(a + b + c) + abc = 0.\\]Since $a + b + c = 1$ and $abc = -\\frac{8}{3},$ this simplifies to\n\\begin{align*}\n3ab(a + b + c) - 3abc(a + b + c) + abc &= 0 \\\\\n3ab(a + b + c) + 8abc &= 0 \\\\\n3ab(1) + 8\\left(-\\frac{8}{3}\\right) &= 0 \\\\\n3ab -\\frac{64}{3} &= 0 \\\\\nab &= \\frac{64}{9}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nAdding in linear terms, we get $ab + ac + bc = ab + ab + ab = 3\\left(\\frac{64}{9}\\right) = \\boxed{\\frac{64}{3}}.$The answer is: \\frac{64}{3}",
