      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We know from Vieta's formulas that the sum of the roots to the equation $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ is $-b/a.$  Thus, the sum of the roots of the equation $3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8 = 0$ is $(-11)/3.$\n\nLet's investigate what happens when we add the equation to itself.  Note that the left side is $0$ and the right side is $(9(a^2+b^2+c^2)),$ $9(ab+bc+ac),$ and $-(3ac).$  Thus, the left side equals the right side if and only if $ab+ac+bc=0.$\n\nTherefore, $ab+ac+bc=\\boxed{0}.$. The answer is 0",
