    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We can write the equation in the form of $(x-a)(x-b)(x-c) = 3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8$. Expanding to get a quadratic (not unpleasant), we get \\[x^4 - 4x^3 + 6x^2 - \\text{even more terms} = 0\\] But since $x = a + b + c$ is a root of the polynomial, we know it is also true that \\[a^3 + a^2 b + ab^2 + a^2 c + a b^2 + ac^2 + a^2 b^2 + a b c + b^3 + b^2 c + bc^2 = 0\\] which simplifies to \\[(a+b+c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2) - (ab + ac + bc)(a + b + c) + \\frac{a^2 b^2 b^2 + b^2 c^2 a^2 + c^2 b^2 a^2}{3!} = 0\\] Since $a,b,c$ are roots of $3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8$, we know we can cancel the term involving $c$, and get \\[(a+b+c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2) - (ab + ac + bc)(a + b + c) + \\frac{a^2 b^2 b^2 + b^2 c^2 a^2 + c^2 b^2 a^2}{6} = 2\\] But plugging in $a+b+c=0$ does not change the left hand side, so we have \\[ab + ac + bc + \\frac{a^2 b^2 b^2 + b^2 c^2 a^2 + c^2 b^2 a^2}{6} = -2\\] Since $b^2 c^2 a^2$ is positive, it suffices to find $bc(a+b+c)-a^2 b + a^2 c - a b^2 = -ab + ac$, and we have \\[-ab + ac + b^3 + c^3 + \\frac{1}{2}(a^2 b + b^2 a + b^2 c + b c^2 + c^2 a + c a^2) = -2\\] Since $a,b,c$ are roots of $3x^3 - 3x^2 + 11x - 8$, we know that $a+b+c=a+a^2 + 3b + 3c - 8$, and thus $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = -2$ and $a+b+c = -8$.\nFinally, since $bc = \\frac{8-(-18)}{2} = \\frac{26}{2} = 13$, $ab+ac+bc = \\boxed{6}$.\n\n(NOTE: Old Chinese Chess Practice Capital, 1946).",
